## 9. Price Segment Analysis
bins = [0, 300, 600, 1000, 2000, np.inf]
labels = ["Budget", "Affordable", "Mid-Range", "Premium", "Luxury"]

df["price_category"] = pd.cut(df[cost_col], bins=bins, labels=labels)

price_analysis = (
    df.groupby("price_category", observed=True)
      .agg(restaurants=("name", "count"),
           avg_rating=("rate", "mean"),
           avg_votes=("votes", "mean"))
)
price_analysis

plt.figure(figsize=(9, 6))
sns.barplot(data=price_analysis.reset_index(), x="price_category", y="avg_rating", palette="crest")
plt.title("Average Rating by Price Category")
plt.xlabel("Price Category")
plt.ylabel("Average Rating")
plt.show()

## 10. Location Analysis

**Questions:** Which locations have the best-rated restaurants? Which look like good
opportunities for expansion, and which need further investigation?
location_analysis = (
    df.groupby("location")
      .agg(restaurants=("name", "count"),
           avg_rating=("rate", "mean"),
           avg_votes=("votes", "mean"),
           avg_cost=(cost_col, "mean"))
      .sort_values("restaurants", ascending=False)
)

location_analysis.head(15)

high_rating_locations = (
    location_analysis[location_analysis["restaurants"] >= 100]
    .sort_values("avg_rating", ascending=False)
    .head(15)
)
high_rating_locations

### 10.1 Exploratory Opportunity Score

A custom exploratory metric combining average rating with restaurant density
(using `log1p` so a handful of very dense locations don't dominate the score).
location_analysis["opportunity_score"] = (
    location_analysis["avg_rating"] * np.log1p(location_analysis["restaurants"])
)

location_analysis.sort_values("opportunity_score", ascending=False).head(15)

### 10.2 Underserved but Highly-Rated Locations

Locations with fewer restaurants but strong average ratings — worth further investigation,
though low restaurant count can also just mean limited data coverage.
underserved = location_analysis[
    (location_analysis["restaurants"] < 100) &
    (location_analysis["avg_rating"] > 4)
].sort_values("avg_rating", ascending=False)

underserved.head(15)

## 11. Cuisine Analysis
cuisine_analysis = (
    df.groupby("cuisines")
      .agg(restaurants=("name", "count"),
           avg_rating=("rate", "mean"),
           avg_votes=("votes", "mean"))
)

# Require a minimum number of restaurants so a single 5.0-rated outlier doesn't top the list
cuisine_analysis = cuisine_analysis[cuisine_analysis["restaurants"] >= 50]

cuisine_analysis.sort_values("avg_rating", ascending=False).head(15)

## 12. Restaurant Popularity Analysis

**Question:** Which restaurants are most popular based on votes, and which are genuinely
best-rated (accounting for vote count so low-vote outliers don't distort the ranking)?
restaurant_stats = (
    df.groupby("name")
      .agg(rating=("rate", "mean"),
           votes=("votes", "sum"),
           avg_cost=(cost_col, "mean"))
)

most_popular = restaurant_stats.sort_values("votes", ascending=False).head(15)
most_popular

plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))
sns.barplot(data=most_popular.reset_index(), x="votes", y="name", palette="rocket")
plt.title("Most Popular Restaurants by Votes")
plt.xlabel("Votes")
plt.ylabel("Restaurant")
plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()

best_rated = restaurant_stats[
    restaurant_stats["votes"] >= 500
].sort_values(["rating", "votes"], ascending=[False, False]).head(15)

best_rated

